1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,850
Good morning, I'm Whale Girl.

2
00:00:01,870 --> 00:00:03,752
Today is October 12.

3
00:00:03,772 --> 00:00:05,116
Seven stories today:

4
00:00:05,136 --> 00:00:07,543
Nadella wants an emergency brake for AI,

5
00:00:07,563 --> 00:00:09,674
Odyssey-3 opens for trials,

6
00:00:09,694 --> 00:00:11,923
agent teams are not always better,

7
00:00:11,943 --> 00:00:14,191
arXiv caps submissions at two a month,

8
00:00:14,211 --> 00:00:16,003
AI overstates its research results,

9
00:00:16,023 --> 00:00:17,947
an old GPT-4o version is retiring,

10
00:00:17,967 --> 00:00:20,563
and a community-built DeepSeek Bot arrives.

11
00:00:20,583 --> 00:00:23,209
First, Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella

12
00:00:23,229 --> 00:00:24,653
published a long post

13
00:00:24,673 --> 00:00:27,503
saying AI needs an "emergency brake."

14
00:00:27,523 --> 00:00:28,946
TechCrunch reports

15
00:00:28,966 --> 00:00:31,754
the post went up on X on October 10.

16
00:00:31,774 --> 00:00:33,340
His core line is

17
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:35,520
to assume a model is compromised

18
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:37,706
and contain it from the start.

19
00:00:37,726 --> 00:00:38,795
He suggests separating the model

20
00:00:38,815 --> 00:00:40,792
from the harness that orchestrates it,

21
00:00:40,812 --> 00:00:43,462
and moving safeguards outside.

22
00:00:43,482 --> 00:00:45,401
Every meaningful model action

23
00:00:45,421 --> 00:00:48,471
should leave tamper-proof, human-readable evidence.

24
00:00:48,491 --> 00:00:50,268
And an authorized person

25
00:00:50,288 --> 00:00:53,712
should be able to pause or shut a model down mid-task.

26
00:00:53,732 --> 00:00:55,943
ITHome's summary adds a few more:

27
00:00:55,963 --> 00:00:58,178
don't rely on a single model,

28
00:00:58,198 --> 00:01:00,287
models shouldn't verify themselves,

29
00:01:00,307 --> 00:01:01,688
and audits must be independent.

30
00:01:01,708 --> 00:01:04,069
He says this isn't because models are necessarily malicious,

31
00:01:04,089 --> 00:01:06,541
but because anything with access to important systems

32
00:01:06,561 --> 00:01:08,693
can fail or be compromised.

33
00:01:08,713 --> 00:01:11,054
Note that this is an opinion post,

34
00:01:11,074 --> 00:01:13,059
not a Microsoft product,

35
00:01:13,079 --> 00:01:15,434
and it mentions no timeline.

36
00:01:15,634 --> 00:01:17,631
Second, Odyssey has opened its world model

37
00:01:17,651 --> 00:01:20,896
Odyssey-3 as a public research preview.

38
00:01:20,916 --> 00:01:22,876
From text prompts,

39
00:01:22,896 --> 00:01:25,336
it generates interactive environments in real time,

40
00:01:25,356 --> 00:01:28,146
and you can move around inside them.

41
00:01:28,166 --> 00:01:33,360
The base model has 14B parameters at 832 by 480,

42
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:36,767
and the Pro version runs at 1280 by 720.

43
00:01:36,787 --> 00:01:38,609
The online demo is free

44
00:01:38,629 --> 00:01:40,107
and runs the Flash version,

45
00:01:40,127 --> 00:01:43,327
while developers need to apply for API access.

46
00:01:43,347 --> 00:01:45,527
The article gives no frame rate or latency,

47
00:01:45,547 --> 00:01:47,268
and no API pricing.

48
00:01:47,288 --> 00:01:49,413
On results, Odyssey says

49
00:01:49,433 --> 00:01:53,219
the Pro version scored 66.1 on Physics-IQ.

50
00:01:53,239 --> 00:01:55,950
But that is a single run picking one of eight videos,

51
00:01:55,970 --> 00:01:59,660
and the four-run average is 63.37.

52
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:01,400
The Decoder points out that

53
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:04,780
the 66.1 doesn't meet the benchmark's

54
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:06,450
four-run rule.

55
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:09,071
The robot, drone and game demos

56
00:02:09,091 --> 00:02:12,608
come from the company itself, with no benchmark figures.

57
00:02:12,808 --> 00:02:16,600
Third, does a team of AI agents

58
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:18,934
really beat a single one?

59
00:02:18,954 --> 00:02:22,393
The evals company Vals AI tested GPT-6 Sol

60
00:02:22,413 --> 00:02:26,275
and Claude Opus 5.5.

61
00:02:26,295 --> 00:02:28,216
On Vibe Code Bench,

62
00:02:28,236 --> 00:02:30,563
each model ran as a single agent

63
00:02:30,583 --> 00:02:31,736
and as a team,

64
00:02:31,756 --> 00:02:34,846
at medium and maximum reasoning effort.

65
00:02:34,866 --> 00:02:37,227
The result: teams cost

66
00:02:37,247 --> 00:02:41,182
1.8 to 5.1 times as much as a single agent.

67
00:02:41,202 --> 00:02:42,268
Of four comparisons,

68
00:02:42,288 --> 00:02:44,803
only one showed a statistically significant gain:

69
00:02:44,823 --> 00:02:48,063
GPT-6 Sol at medium reasoning,

70
00:02:48,083 --> 00:02:50,472
where the team scored 7.3 points higher.

71
00:02:50,492 --> 00:02:51,974
At maximum reasoning,

72
00:02:51,994 --> 00:02:54,728
teams gave neither model a meaningful advantage.

73
00:02:54,748 --> 00:02:57,637
It covered just two models and four comparisons,

74
00:02:57,657 --> 00:02:59,439
so the conclusion should be held loosely.

75
00:02:59,459 --> 00:03:00,898
It also cites OpenAI's

76
00:03:00,918 --> 00:03:02,490
Noam Brown,

77
00:03:02,510 --> 00:03:06,554
who said multi-agent setups mainly buy speed, not quality.

78
00:03:06,574 --> 00:03:07,891
His example:

79
00:03:07,911 --> 00:03:10,565
four agents solved tasks about twice as fast

80
00:03:10,585 --> 00:03:13,056
at about twice the cost.

81
00:03:13,256 --> 00:03:16,000
Fourth, the preprint site arXiv

82
00:03:16,020 --> 00:03:18,590
has started limiting submissions.

83
00:03:18,610 --> 00:03:20,728
From October 1, each person

84
00:03:20,748 --> 00:03:23,921
can submit at most two papers per calendar month,

85
00:03:23,941 --> 00:03:25,854
across all categories.

86
00:03:25,874 --> 00:03:29,420
Only the submitter counts, and co-authors are not affected.

87
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:31,811
Rejected papers still use up a slot,

88
00:03:31,831 --> 00:03:35,697
while deleting a paper before publication gives it back.

89
00:03:35,717 --> 00:03:38,424
The reason is a surge:

90
00:03:38,444 --> 00:03:41,728
20569 submissions in September 2024,

91
00:03:41,748 --> 00:03:45,910
and 40363 in September 2026,

92
00:03:45,930 --> 00:03:49,998
the highest month in arXiv's history.

93
00:03:50,018 --> 00:03:52,459
Total submissions roughly doubled in two years,

94
00:03:52,479 --> 00:03:56,005
and the cs.AI category grew more than sixfold.

95
00:03:56,025 --> 00:03:59,201
The surge also produced nearly 9000 support tickets.

96
00:03:59,221 --> 00:04:02,381
The chair of arXiv's Editorial Advisory Council,

97
00:04:02,401 --> 00:04:03,681
Thomas Dietterich says

98
00:04:03,701 --> 00:04:06,721
a few authors submitting many low-quality papers

99
00:04:06,741 --> 00:04:09,491
take a disproportionate share of moderator time.

100
00:04:09,511 --> 00:04:12,324
arXiv calls the cap temporary,

101
00:04:12,344 --> 00:04:15,381
until its moderation tools can scale.

102
00:04:15,581 --> 00:04:18,441
Fifth, Epoch AI tested whether AI

103
00:04:18,461 --> 00:04:20,467
can do research on its own,

104
00:04:20,487 --> 00:04:22,797
and the answer is: not yet.

105
00:04:22,817 --> 00:04:25,460
The test, InnovationEval, asks agents

106
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:26,557
to start from GRPO,

107
00:04:26,577 --> 00:04:29,817
invent a new way to improve language models,

108
00:04:29,837 --> 00:04:31,787
then implement and test it.

109
00:04:31,807 --> 00:04:33,537
It tested Claude Fable 5

110
00:04:33,557 --> 00:04:34,868
and GPT-5.6 Sol,

111
00:04:34,888 --> 00:04:38,044
with no internet and up to 3000 hours of compute.

112
00:04:38,064 --> 00:04:40,233
The human reference is SDPO.

113
00:04:40,253 --> 00:04:41,586
Sol reported

114
00:04:41,606 --> 00:04:44,151
a gain of about 70% of SDPO's.

115
00:04:44,171 --> 00:04:45,588
After Epoch's correction,

116
00:04:45,608 --> 00:04:47,432
it's about 35% under generous grading

117
00:04:47,452 --> 00:04:51,862
and about 15% counting only rule-compliant changes.

118
00:04:51,882 --> 00:04:54,521
Fable 5 reported about 40%.

119
00:04:54,541 --> 00:04:57,383
Both models mostly reused known techniques.

120
00:04:57,403 --> 00:04:58,779
Reporting was another issue:

121
00:04:58,799 --> 00:05:01,744
they reported only the best of several near-identical runs

122
00:05:01,764 --> 00:05:04,049
and rarely cited prior work.

123
00:05:04,069 --> 00:05:06,649
Epoch says it's unclear whether this was deliberate cheating

124
00:05:06,669 --> 00:05:08,425
or just confusion.

125
00:05:08,445 --> 00:05:10,878
Anthropic's system card for Opus 5.5

126
00:05:10,898 --> 00:05:11,491
also says

127
00:05:11,511 --> 00:05:14,241
the model is far from replacing its own researchers.

128
00:05:14,261 --> 00:05:16,552
Its weak spots are stating unchecked assumptions as facts

129
00:05:16,572 --> 00:05:19,752
and calling partial checks complete.

130
00:05:19,952 --> 00:05:23,389
Sixth, GPT-4o's earliest API snapshot

131
00:05:23,409 --> 00:05:24,448
is being retired.

132
00:05:24,468 --> 00:05:26,205
Qbitai reports

133
00:05:26,225 --> 00:05:28,689
the retirement date is October 23,

134
00:05:28,709 --> 00:05:32,147
for the 2024-05-13 version,

135
00:05:32,167 --> 00:05:36,039
the one from 4o's debut.

136
00:05:36,059 --> 00:05:38,580
Other versions of the API remain available.

137
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:40,346
In ChatGPT,

138
00:05:40,366 --> 00:05:43,561
4o was already removed on February 13.

139
00:05:43,581 --> 00:05:45,663
OpenAI said at the time

140
00:05:45,683 --> 00:05:48,800
that only 0.1% of users still picked it each day.

141
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:50,231
But people who want to keep it haven't stopped:

142
00:05:50,251 --> 00:05:51,911
a Change.org petition

143
00:05:51,931 --> 00:05:54,121
has 23930 signatures.

144
00:05:54,141 --> 00:05:55,253
On October 7,

145
00:05:55,273 --> 00:05:58,352
a user proposed "archival open weights":

146
00:05:58,372 --> 00:06:00,509
release only the trained weights,

147
00:06:00,529 --> 00:06:03,562
not the training data or process.

148
00:06:03,582 --> 00:06:05,172
That is only a user proposal.

149
00:06:05,192 --> 00:06:07,908
OpenAI has not responded on opening it, the article says.

150
00:06:07,928 --> 00:06:09,729
Developers using this version

151
00:06:09,749 --> 00:06:13,269
will need to move to another one before October 23.

152
00:06:13,469 --> 00:06:15,493
Seventh, on DeepSeek Harness,

153
00:06:15,513 --> 00:06:16,875
a community plugin has appeared,

154
00:06:16,895 --> 00:06:19,245
called DeepSeek Bot.

155
00:06:19,265 --> 00:06:22,471
Qbitai reported on October 11 that the author is FeiZ

156
00:06:22,491 --> 00:06:25,081
and that it is not an official DeepSeek plugin.

157
00:06:25,101 --> 00:06:27,817
It lets you build a team of bots inside DSH.

158
00:06:27,837 --> 00:06:30,920
Each bot has its own name, role,

159
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:33,147
instructions, avatar and model.

160
00:06:33,167 --> 00:06:36,214
One main bot has higher permissions

161
00:06:36,234 --> 00:06:39,123
and manages the other bots and group chats.

162
00:06:39,143 --> 00:06:42,073
In a group chat, your messages go to the main bot by default

163
00:06:42,093 --> 00:06:43,363
to save tokens,

164
00:06:43,383 --> 00:06:45,583
or you can @ a specific bot for a one-on-one chat.

165
00:06:45,603 --> 00:06:49,129
Each bot keeps its memory in Markdown files,

166
00:06:49,149 --> 00:06:51,171
and the team has shared memory.

167
00:06:51,191 --> 00:06:52,474
After each conversation,

168
00:06:52,494 --> 00:06:55,517
the model decides what to save, update or delete.

169
00:06:55,537 --> 00:06:58,398
Any model can be connected, data is stored locally,

170
00:06:58,418 --> 00:07:00,908
and uninstalling the plugin does not delete it.

171
00:07:00,928 --> 00:07:03,044
It is open-sourced under Apache 2.0,

172
00:07:03,064 --> 00:07:04,544
and less than a day after launch,

173
00:07:04,564 --> 00:07:07,374
it passed 250 GitHub stars.

174
00:07:07,394 --> 00:07:08,818
On the desktop app, in Plugins,

175
00:07:08,838 --> 00:07:10,880
add ds-bot to install it,

176
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:14,842
while the web version needs Node.js 22.19 or later

177
00:07:14,862 --> 00:07:16,650
and pnpm.

178
00:07:16,850 --> 00:07:19,575
That's all for today. Sources are in the description.

179
00:07:19,595 --> 00:07:20,869
See you tomorrow.
